TSDataSet Name=FRM_EObs
Description=Ensemble version of FRM-Only regime. Simulates many different flood events with different inflows and starting pool. See ../3.7_Synthetics_ResSim_Inputs for a legend on what each run is. 

FRM Baseline Conditions for Willamette FIRO Study. 3 hour timestep suitable for flood event modeling. 

Operations reflect FRM Baseline conditions (see documentation).

Make sure to turn all DSS output off if you want to run a full ensemble on one computer. 
Otherwise, the output DSS file will get huge and ResSim will choke.

To run this simulation, we have to "trick" ResSim a little bit.
If we created a simulation traditionally, 
Ressim will try to extract all timeseries for every ensemble event from the input .dss file with all inflows/elevations.
This would take a long time.
Rather than doing that, it is best to do the following:
1. Set up the ensemble alternative so it is only referencing a single event, like "000001", in the "Ensemble" tab.
2. Create the simulation:
	Start date: 01Jan3000 1200
	Lookback: 01Jan3000 0300
	End Date: 16Jan3000 0000
3. In the simulation module, change the "Ensemble" tab of the alternative back to what it should be (e.g. "000001-0017187")
4. Close down ResSim
5. Copy the input .dss file with all inputs needed to the "../rss/$SIM_NAME$" folder. This is usually from "../3.7_Synthetics_ResSim_Inputs\out"
	Rename it "simulation.dss". Delete the existing "simulation.dss" file.
6. Re-open ResSim and then run
7. When running multi-thread ensemble compute, Ressim will usually fail before it gets all the way through:
	Not exactly sure why this happens, likely some memory issue
	Doesn't have a problem if running single-thread, but much slower.
8. After the run fails, run the "../scripts/Utilities/PrintMissingEnsembles.py" script.
	It spits out a string showing all the runs that failed, e.g. "000069-001075, 001084, ..."
9. Copy this text of the failed runs into the "Ensemble" tab in ResSim in the simulation, and run again
10. Repeat as many times as needed until all ensemble members are completed. 
11. The results for each simulation are stored in the "../rss/$SIM_NAME$/PeakExtract.csv", which is appended with every completed ensemble member.
	If you need to re-run a simulation, delete out this file first, otherwise we'll have duplicates in the file. (Observed TS Data Set)

